  "question": {
    "html": "<p>The graph shows how the net torque varies with time on a wheel that lies in the horizontal plane. The wheel is initially at rest and is free to rotate about its central axis. </p>\n\n<figure data-asset-id=\"fig_01\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 1\" src=\"asset:fig_01\"/></figure>\n\n<p>After the first <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"45\\; \\textup{ms}\"></span>, no external torque acts on the wheel and it eventually comes to rest due to a constant, applied decelerating force, while completing a total of four full rotations. The mass of the wheel is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"480\\; \\textup{kg}\"></span>, the diameter of the wheel is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"10\\; \\textup{m}\"></span>, and the moment of inertia of the wheel is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"I = \\frac{1}{2}MR^{2}\"></span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"M\"></span> is the mass and <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"R\"></span> is the radius of the wheel.</p>\n<p>What is the best estimate for the magnitude of the angular deceleration of the wheel during the time when no external torque acts on it?</p>\n<p></p>\n<ol class=\"mcq-options\">\n<li data-label=\"A\"><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"0.020\\; \\textup{rad}\\textup{s}^{-2}\"></span></li>\n<li data-label=\"B\"><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"0.13\\; \\textup{rad}\\textup{s}^{-2}\"></span></li>\n<li data-label=\"C\"><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"0.32\\; \\textup{rad}\\textup{s}^{-2}\"></span></li>\n<li data-label=\"D\"><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"2.0\\; \\textup{rad}\\textup{s}^{-2}\"></span></li>\n</ol>"
  },
